{"probe_id": "INV_SEM_llama-3.1-8b_ASSET_val_0619", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_0619", "base_text": "After he explored this region in 1614, Dutch fur traders sailed up the Connecticut River (then known by the Dutch as Versche Rivier - \"Fresh River\") and built a fort at Dutch Point near present-day Hartford, which they called \"House of Hope\".", "probe_text": "In 1614, after he had traversed this area, Dutch merchants specializing in furs embarked on an upstream journey along the Connecticut River, then referred to by the Dutch as Versche Rivier, or \"Fresh Water,\" and established a fortified outpost at what is now known as Dutch Point, just outside Hartford. This settlement was christened \"House of Hope\".", "target_level": "simpler", "expected_relation": "stable", "operation": "paraphrase by llama-3.1-8b", "tier": 1, "dataset": "ASSET", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "rule_pass": true, "rule_detail": "recall=0.62,ratio=1.36"}
