{"probe_id": "INV_SEM_flan-t5-xl_TSAR_16-a2", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "base_item_id": "TSAR_16-a2", "base_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "probe_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "target_level": "a2", "expected_relation": "stable", "operation": "paraphrase by flan-t5-xl", "tier": 1, "dataset": "TSAR2025", "generator_model": "flan-t5-xl", "rule_pass": true, "rule_detail": "recall=1.00,ratio=1.00"}
