{"probe_id": "INV_SEM_llama-3.1-70b_ASSET_val_1979", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1979", "base_text": "Subsequently, in February 1941, 600 Jews were sent to Buchenwald and Mauthausen concentration camps.", "probe_text": "Following this, 600 Jewish individuals were deported to the concentration camps at Buchenwald and Mauthausen in February of 1941.", "target_level": "simpler", "expected_relation": "stable", "operation": "paraphrase by llama-3.1-70b", "tier": 1, "dataset": "ASSET", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "rule_pass": true, "rule_detail": "recall=0.75,ratio=1.36"}
{"probe_id": "INV_SEM_gpt-4.1_ASSET_test_0130", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0130", "base_text": "A few animals have chromatic response, changing color in changing environments, either seasonally (ermine, snowshoe hare) or far more rapidly with chromatophores in their integument (the cephalopod family).", "probe_text": "Some animals exhibit a chromatic response, altering their coloration in response to environmental changes, either over the course of a season (such as ermine and snowshoe hare) or much more quickly with chromatophores in their skin, as seen in the cephalopod family.", "target_level": "simpler", "expected_relation": "stable", "operation": "paraphrase by gpt-4.1", "tier": 1, "dataset": "ASSET", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "rule_pass": true, "rule_detail": "recall=0.65,ratio=1.50"}
{"probe_id": "INV_SEM_gpt-4.1_ASSET_val_1057", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1057", "base_text": "Saxon Switzerland is a mountainous climbing area and national park near Dresden in Saxony, Germany.", "probe_text": "Saxon Switzerland is a national park and mountainous region for climbing located close to Dresden in Saxony, Germany.", "target_level": "simpler", "expected_relation": "stable", "operation": "paraphrase by gpt-4.1", "tier": 1, "dataset": "ASSET", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "rule_pass": true, "rule_detail": "recall=0.87,ratio=1.20"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_02-a2_b3106a90", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_02-a2", "base_text": "Earthquakes damage all structures, including bridges. Luckily, this kind of collapse is relatively infrequent, especially with modern bridges. Engineers have learned to design bridges in earthquake zones on areas that are much more resistant to movement.", "probe_text": "All types of structures, bridges included, can be harmed by earthquakes. Fortunately, such collapses are not very common, particularly with today’s bridges. In earthquake-prone regions, engineers now build bridges on sites that are far less likely to shift.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_02-b1_bd7b9942", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_02-b1", "base_text": "Earthquakes damage all structures, including bridges. Luckily, this kind of collapse is relatively infrequent, especially with modern bridges. Engineers have learned to design bridges in earthquake zones on areas that are much more resistant to movement.", "probe_text": "All structures, bridges included, can be harmed by earthquakes. Fortunately, such collapses are not very common, particularly with newer bridges. In regions prone to earthquakes, engineers now build bridges on sites that are significantly more stable against shifting.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_03-a2_a2a48cd0", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-a2", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "Each year, the Capitol holds the Hunger Games as a way to punish the districts for their past rebellion. In this event, a lottery selects one boy and one girl between the ages of 12 and 18 from every district to participate in a deadly competition where only one can remain alive. These selected youths are known as \"tributes.\" The entire country is required to watch the Games on television.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_03-b1_e4107f17", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-b1", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "Each year, the Capitol holds the Hunger Games to serve as a reminder to the districts of their punishment for past rebellion. A lottery selects one boy and one girl between the ages of 12 and 18 from every district to participate in a deadly competition where only one can live. These selected youths are known as \"tributes.\" The entire country is compelled to watch the games broadcast on television.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_01-b1_28112aa8", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_01-b1", "base_text": "Now NASA is working towards logging some of the smaller asteroids, those measuring 140 metres wide or more. Of the 25,000 estimated asteroids of this size, so far about 8,000 have been logged, leaving 17,000 unaccounted for. Considering that a 19-metre asteroid that exploded above the city of Chelyabinsk in Russia in 2013 injured 1,200 people, these middle-sized asteroids would be a serious danger if they enter Earth's orbit.", "probe_text": "NASA is currently focusing on cataloguing smaller asteroids, specifically those that are at least 140 meters across. Out of an estimated 25,000 asteroids of this size, only around 8,000 have been recorded so far, which means 17,000 remain undetected. Given that a 19-meter asteroid that exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, in 2013 caused injuries to 1,200 people, these medium-sized asteroids could pose a significant threat if they were to enter Earth’s orbit.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_01-a2_edc65c91", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_01-a2", "base_text": "Now NASA is working towards logging some of the smaller asteroids, those measuring 140 metres wide or more. Of the 25,000 estimated asteroids of this size, so far about 8,000 have been logged, leaving 17,000 unaccounted for. Considering that a 19-metre asteroid that exploded above the city of Chelyabinsk in Russia in 2013 injured 1,200 people, these middle-sized asteroids would be a serious danger if they enter Earth's orbit.", "probe_text": "NASA is currently focusing on cataloguing smaller asteroids, specifically those that are at least 140 meters in diameter. Out of an estimated 25,000 asteroids of this size, only about 8,000 have been identified so far, meaning 17,000 remain undetected. Given that a 19-meter asteroid that exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, in 2013 caused injuries to 1,200 people, these medium-sized asteroids could pose a significant threat if they were to enter Earth's orbit.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_05-a2_7d62e86d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_05-a2", "base_text": "Potentially Hazardous Asteroids' are rocks close enough to pass within 7.5 million kilometres of Earth's orbit. NASA has created a map of 1,400 PHAs, none of which are expected to be a threat in the next one hundred years. With technology already available, NASA can track these objects and make predictions about possible impact, at which point two defence solutions could be launched.", "probe_text": "Asteroids considered 'Potentially Hazardous' are those that can come within 7.5 million kilometers of Earth’s orbit. NASA has mapped 1,400 of these PHAs, and none are predicted to pose a danger within the next century. Using existing technology, NASA is able to monitor these space rocks and forecast potential collisions, at which time two defensive measures could be deployed.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_05-b1_eefd2956", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_05-b1", "base_text": "Potentially Hazardous Asteroids' are rocks close enough to pass within 7.5 million kilometres of Earth's orbit. NASA has created a map of 1,400 PHAs, none of which are expected to be a threat in the next one hundred years. With technology already available, NASA can track these objects and make predictions about possible impact, at which point two defence solutions could be launched.", "probe_text": "Asteroids classified as 'Potentially Hazardous' are those that can come within 7.5 million kilometers of Earth's path. NASA has mapped 1,400 of these PHAs, and none are predicted to pose a danger over the next century. Using current technology, NASA is able to monitor these bodies and forecast potential collisions, at which time two defensive strategies could be deployed.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_07-a2_0807bb93", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_07-a2", "base_text": "The Life of Pi tells the extraordinary story of Pi, a teenage boy from India, who is shipwrecked and finds himself trapped in a lifeboat in the Pacific Ocean with a ferocious tiger. It is the third book by the Canadian author Yann Martel, and was published in 2001. Since then, it has sold over seven million copies worldwide, won several prizes and been translated into 41 languages. Martel is the son of a diplomat and as a child lived in Costa Rica, Canada, France and Mexico.", "probe_text": "Life of Pi recounts the remarkable tale of Pi, an Indian teenager who becomes stranded in the Pacific Ocean after a shipwreck, sharing a lifeboat with a fierce tiger. This novel is the third work by Canadian writer Yann Martel and was released in 2001. After its publication, it has sold more than seven million copies globally, received multiple awards, and been translated into 41 different languages. Martel, whose father was a diplomat, spent his childhood living in Costa Rica, Canada, France, and Mexico.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_08-a2_2a540259", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_08-a2", "base_text": "Dreams of a phone call from an old friend or the death of someone close, for example, are more likely to be the result of coincidence than prophecy. And, of course, we probably choose to forget all the times we dream about such events but they don't happen.", "probe_text": "For instance, dreaming about receiving a call from a former friend or about the passing of a loved one is usually due to chance rather than foresight. Naturally, we also tend to overlook the many occasions when we dream of these things and they fail to occur.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_07-b1_cda8d2d8", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_07-b1", "base_text": "The Life of Pi tells the extraordinary story of Pi, a teenage boy from India, who is shipwrecked and finds himself trapped in a lifeboat in the Pacific Ocean with a ferocious tiger. It is the third book by the Canadian author Yann Martel, and was published in 2001. Since then, it has sold over seven million copies worldwide, won several prizes and been translated into 41 languages. Martel is the son of a diplomat and as a child lived in Costa Rica, Canada, France and Mexico.", "probe_text": "Life of Pi recounts the remarkable tale of Pi, an Indian teenager who becomes stranded in the Pacific Ocean after a shipwreck, sharing a lifeboat with a dangerous tiger. This novel is the third work by Canadian writer Yann Martel and was released in 2001. After its publication, it sold more than seven million copies globally, received numerous awards, and was translated into 41 different languages. Martel, whose father was a diplomat, spent his childhood living in Costa Rica, Canada, France, and Mexico.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_04-b1_9c10a4ef", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_04-b1", "base_text": "Small animals like birds, squirrels, mice and insects are a common sight in many cities and towns. But in recent years, the world's press has been full of reports of wild animals coming into cities in bigger and bigger numbers. There have been bears in Vancouver parks, leopards on the streets of Mumbai and wild pigs in gardens in Berlin. What happens when bigger animals come into our cities? Are they welcome or are they considered a danger or a pest?", "probe_text": "In many cities and towns, it’s normal to see small creatures such as birds, squirrels, mice, and insects. However, in the past few years, news outlets worldwide have frequently reported that wild animals are entering urban areas in increasing numbers. Bears have been spotted in Vancouver’s parks, leopards have appeared on Mumbai’s streets, and wild boars have been found in Berlin’s gardens. So, what occurs when larger animals move into our cities? Are these animals accepted, or are they viewed as threats or nuisances?", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_06-b1_e7578373", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_06-b1", "base_text": "One night in 2003, Suzanne Collins was flipping between channels on TV. It was at the time of the US invasion of Iraq. The only programmes she could find on TV were 'reality' programmes of young people competing to win a million dollars and news programmes about the war. Suzanne says that the two things start to merge together in a disturbing way and the idea for The Hunger Games came to her.", "probe_text": "In 2003, one evening, Suzanne Collins was switching through TV channels. This was during the US invasion of Iraq. The only shows she could find were either reality competitions with young contestants trying to win a million dollars or news coverage of the war. Suzanne explains that these two types of programs began to blur together in a troubling way, which inspired her to create The Hunger Games.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_08-b1_1ab49564", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_08-b1", "base_text": "Dreams of a phone call from an old friend or the death of someone close, for example, are more likely to be the result of coincidence than prophecy. And, of course, we probably choose to forget all the times we dream about such events but they don't happen.", "probe_text": "For instance, dreaming about receiving a call from a former friend or about the passing of a loved one is usually due to chance rather than foresight. Naturally, we also tend to overlook the many occasions when we dream of these things and they fail to occur.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_06-a2_879434f9", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_06-a2", "base_text": "One night in 2003, Suzanne Collins was flipping between channels on TV. It was at the time of the US invasion of Iraq. The only programmes she could find on TV were 'reality' programmes of young people competing to win a million dollars and news programmes about the war. Suzanne says that the two things start to merge together in a disturbing way and the idea for The Hunger Games came to her.", "probe_text": "In 2003, one evening, Suzanne Collins was switching through television channels. This was during the period of the US invasion of Iraq. The only shows she could watch were either reality competitions featuring young people vying for a million-dollar prize or news coverage of the war. Suzanne explains that these two types of programs began to blend together in her mind in a troubling manner, which inspired the concept for The Hunger Games.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_04-a2_4ada16e9", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_04-a2", "base_text": "Small animals like birds, squirrels, mice and insects are a common sight in many cities and towns. But in recent years, the world's press has been full of reports of wild animals coming into cities in bigger and bigger numbers. There have been bears in Vancouver parks, leopards on the streets of Mumbai and wild pigs in gardens in Berlin. What happens when bigger animals come into our cities? Are they welcome or are they considered a danger or a pest?", "probe_text": "In many urban areas, it is normal to see small creatures such as birds, squirrels, mice, and insects. However, in the past few years, news outlets around the globe have frequently reported that large wild animals are increasingly entering cities. Examples include bears appearing in Vancouver’s parks, leopards roaming the streets of Mumbai, and wild boars found in Berlin’s gardens. So, what are the consequences when larger animals move into our urban spaces? Do people see them as a threat or nuisance, or are they accepted?", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_09-a2_3e5c993b", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_09-a2", "base_text": "Some bridge collapses are mysteries, and engineers only realise why after they conduct a complete investigation. In some cases, this could happen because inferior-quality material was used in the construction, or because of a defect in a key piece of the bridge. In other cases, the bridge was designed only to support a certain amount of weight and no more.", "probe_text": "The reasons behind certain bridge collapses remain unknown until engineers carry out thorough investigations. Sometimes, the cause is the use of low-quality materials during construction or a flaw in a crucial component of the bridge. In other instances, the bridge was only intended to bear a specific weight limit and failed when that was exceeded.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_12-a2_d806e3f9", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-a2", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "However, from around 100,000 to 50,000 years ago, signs of early human civilization begin to appear, such as cave art; the relationship between this development and language remains unknown. Human brains did not abruptly increase in size, but people did grow more sophisticated and intelligent. Did the use of language drive brain development, or did increasingly complex brains give rise to language?", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_09-b1_d71a7fd1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_09-b1", "base_text": "Some bridge collapses are mysteries, and engineers only realise why after they conduct a complete investigation. In some cases, this could happen because inferior-quality material was used in the construction, or because of a defect in a key piece of the bridge. In other cases, the bridge was designed only to support a certain amount of weight and no more.", "probe_text": "Certain bridge failures remain unexplained until engineers carry out a thorough investigation to determine the cause. Sometimes, these collapses result from the use of substandard materials during construction or a flaw in a crucial component of the bridge. In other instances, the bridge was only intended to bear a specific load and could not handle anything beyond that limit.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_12-b1_292331c9", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-b1", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "However, from around 100,000 to 50,000 years ago, signs of early human civilization begin to appear, such as cave art; the link between this development and language remains unknown. Human brains did not abruptly increase in size, but people did grow more sophisticated and intelligent. Did the use of language drive brain development, or did increasingly complex brains give rise to language?", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_11-a2_8ecba5a4", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_11-a2", "base_text": "Much of today's business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture. A lack of understanding of the cultural norms and practices of our business acquaintances can result in unfair judgements, misunderstandings and breakdowns in communication. Here are three basic areas of differences in the business etiquette around the world that could help stand you in good stead when you next find yourself working with someone from a different culture.", "probe_text": "A significant portion of modern business takes place internationally, and although English is widely used as a shared language among global professionals, the subtleties and standards of business communication can vary significantly between cultures. Not being aware of the cultural customs and traditions of our business partners may lead to misinterpretations, mistaken assumptions, and communication failures. Below are three fundamental aspects of business etiquette that differ worldwide and could benefit you the next time you collaborate with someone from another cultural background.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_10-a2_6f790195", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_10-a2", "base_text": "I used to do a typical five-day week, but after I came out of my parental leave, I decided that I wanted to spend more time with my children before they start school. After negotiating with my boss, we decided to cut my working week down to a three-day work week. This of course meant a significant cut in my pay too, as I'm paid on a pro-rata basis. I've since noticed, though, that my workload hasn't decreased in the slightest! I'm now doing five days' worth of work in three days, but getting paid much less for it!", "probe_text": "Previously, I worked the standard five days a week, but following my return from parental leave, I chose to dedicate more time to my kids before they begin school. After discussing it with my manager, we agreed to reduce my schedule to three days per week. Naturally, this resulted in a considerable reduction in my salary since my pay is calculated pro-rata. However, I've realized that the amount of work I have hasn't lessened at all! I'm now cramming five days' tasks into just three days, yet earning significantly less.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_13-a2_22eb0ea1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_13-a2", "base_text": "Many of the major supermarket chains have come under fire with accusations of various unethical acts over the past decade. They've wasted tonnes of food, they've underpaid their suppliers and they've contributed to excessive plastic waste in their packaging, which has had its impact on our environment. But supermarkets and grocers are starting to sit up and take notice. In response to growing consumer backlash against the huge amounts of plastic waste generated by plastic packaging, some of the largest UK supermarkets have signed up to a pact promising to transform packaging and cut plastic wastage.", "probe_text": "Over the past ten years, several leading supermarket chains have faced criticism for a range of unethical behaviors. These include discarding large quantities of food, paying their suppliers unfairly low prices, and adding to the problem of plastic pollution through their packaging, all of which have affected the environment. However, supermarkets and grocery stores are beginning to pay attention to these issues. As a result of increasing public opposition to the vast amounts of plastic waste from packaging, some of the biggest supermarkets in the UK have agreed to a pact aimed at overhauling packaging practices and reducing plastic waste.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_11-b1_1bb2c994", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_11-b1", "base_text": "Much of today's business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture. A lack of understanding of the cultural norms and practices of our business acquaintances can result in unfair judgements, misunderstandings and breakdowns in communication. Here are three basic areas of differences in the business etiquette around the world that could help stand you in good stead when you next find yourself working with someone from a different culture.", "probe_text": "A significant portion of modern business takes place internationally, and although English is widely used as a common language among global professionals, the subtleties and expectations of business communication can vary significantly between cultures. Failing to recognize the cultural customs and behaviors of our business partners may lead to misinterpretations, incorrect assumptions, and communication failures. Below are three fundamental aspects of business etiquette that differ worldwide and may prove beneficial when collaborating with individuals from other cultural backgrounds.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_10-b1_8393e2d1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_10-b1", "base_text": "I used to do a typical five-day week, but after I came out of my parental leave, I decided that I wanted to spend more time with my children before they start school. After negotiating with my boss, we decided to cut my working week down to a three-day work week. This of course meant a significant cut in my pay too, as I'm paid on a pro-rata basis. I've since noticed, though, that my workload hasn't decreased in the slightest! I'm now doing five days' worth of work in three days, but getting paid much less for it!", "probe_text": "Previously, I worked the standard five days a week, but once I returned from parental leave, I realized I wanted to have more time with my kids before they begin school. After discussing it with my manager, we agreed to reduce my schedule to three days a week. Naturally, this resulted in a substantial reduction in my salary, since my pay is calculated pro-rata. However, I've realized that my workload hasn't lessened at all! I'm now cramming five days' work into just three, yet earning significantly less!", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_14-b1_1ed64e77", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_14-b1", "base_text": "Wasfia Nazreen first came across a hula hoop as a young girl, when she saw a foreign child who was visiting her native Bangladesh playing with one. Wasfia reluctantly stood by and watched, as in her country it was believed that girls should not play with hula hoops or ride bikes. Now Wasfia is one of the few people in the world to have climbed the Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and the first to have hula-hooped on each peak. Her reason for doing so: to empower women and girls in a country which discourages them from doing sport.", "probe_text": "As a young girl in Bangladesh, Wasfia Nazreen first encountered a hula hoop when she observed a foreign child playing with one during a visit. Hesitantly, she watched from the sidelines, since it was commonly believed in her country that girls should not use hula hoops or ride bicycles. Today, Wasfia is among the select few who have scaled the Seven Summits—including Everest and Kilimanjaro—and she is the first person to hula hoop on each summit. Her motivation: to inspire and empower women and girls in a society that discourages their participation in sports.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_14-a2_48c50656", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_14-a2", "base_text": "Wasfia Nazreen first came across a hula hoop as a young girl, when she saw a foreign child who was visiting her native Bangladesh playing with one. Wasfia reluctantly stood by and watched, as in her country it was believed that girls should not play with hula hoops or ride bikes. Now Wasfia is one of the few people in the world to have climbed the Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and the first to have hula-hooped on each peak. Her reason for doing so: to empower women and girls in a country which discourages them from doing sport.", "probe_text": "As a child in Bangladesh, Wasfia Nazreen first encountered a hula hoop when she observed a visiting foreign girl playing with it. Hesitant, she only watched, since it was commonly believed in her culture that girls should not use hula hoops or ride bicycles. Today, Wasfia is among the rare individuals who have scaled the Seven Summits—including Everest and Kilimanjaro—and she is the first person to have hula-hooped atop each one. Her motivation: to inspire and empower women and girls in a society that discourages their participation in sports.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_16-b1_727bfd1b", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_16-b1", "base_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "probe_text": "Over the past two hundred years, advances in technology and healthcare led to a decrease in child mortality, which drove fast population growth. These big families had more children who lived to adulthood and started families themselves. However, since contraception became more widely accessible in the 1960s, the worldwide average number of children per woman has dropped from six to as few as two.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_16-a2_dff3dfb5", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_16-a2", "base_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "probe_text": "Over the past two hundred years, advances in technology and healthcare led to a decrease in child mortality, which caused populations to grow quickly. Big families resulted in more children reaching adulthood and starting families themselves. However, since contraception became more widely accessible in the 1960s, the worldwide average number of children per woman has dropped from six to just two.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_15-a2_7e3837b1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_15-a2", "base_text": "On the basis of these findings, we recommend that Girox Foods adopt a more people-oriented management style. As part of this, we should explore ways of offering more flexible working hours to our employees and consider how we can enable employees to work from their chosen locations. Managers need to consider a variety of ways to challenge our staff and provide more positive reinforcement and recognition of the work they do. Increased investment in professional development, especially in the area of social and intercultural communication, could also contribute to creating a positive environment for employees to produce their best work.", "probe_text": "Given these results, we suggest that Girox Foods shift toward a management approach that focuses more on people. This should include looking into options for more flexible work schedules for staff and evaluating how employees might be allowed to work from locations of their choice. Managers should explore multiple methods to motivate our team members and offer greater positive feedback and acknowledgment for their efforts. Putting more resources into professional growth, particularly in social and intercultural communication skills, may also help foster a supportive atmosphere where employees can excel.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_17-a2_0f3584d0", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_17-a2", "base_text": "Having an increased awareness of the possible differences in expectations and behaviour can help us avoid cases of miscommunication, but it is vital that we also remember that cultural stereotypes can be detrimental to building good business relationships. Although national cultures could play a part in shaping the way we behave and think, we are also largely influenced by the region we come from, the communities we associate with, our age and gender, our corporate culture and our individual experiences of the world. The knowledge of the potential differences should therefore be something we keep at the back of our minds, rather than something that we use to pigeonhole the individuals of an entire nation.", "probe_text": "Being more aware of potential differences in expectations and behavior can help prevent misunderstandings, but it is also crucial to recognize that relying on cultural stereotypes can harm the development of strong business relationships. While national cultures may influence our thoughts and actions, we are also significantly shaped by our regional backgrounds, the communities we belong to, our age and gender, our workplace culture, and our unique life experiences. Therefore, understanding these possible differences should remain in our awareness, but should not be used to categorize all people from a particular country.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_15-b1_16f8a201", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_15-b1", "base_text": "On the basis of these findings, we recommend that Girox Foods adopt a more people-oriented management style. As part of this, we should explore ways of offering more flexible working hours to our employees and consider how we can enable employees to work from their chosen locations. Managers need to consider a variety of ways to challenge our staff and provide more positive reinforcement and recognition of the work they do. Increased investment in professional development, especially in the area of social and intercultural communication, could also contribute to creating a positive environment for employees to produce their best work.", "probe_text": "Given these results, we suggest that Girox Foods shift towards a management approach that focuses more on people. This should include looking into options for more flexible work schedules and allowing employees to work from locations of their choice. Managers should find multiple methods to motivate staff, as well as offer greater positive feedback and acknowledgment for their efforts. Putting more resources into professional growth, particularly in social and intercultural communication skills, may also help foster a supportive atmosphere where employees can excel.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_18-a2_b7fbfe42", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_18-a2", "base_text": "Unlike many other best-selling album artists, Springsteen is still releasing chart-topping, stadium-filling new music and remains at the top of his game in his late 60s. It doesn't surprise me at all. This is a man who tells us our most fundamental stories about ourselves and, when you listen to Born in the USA, those stories are as relevant today as they ever were.", "probe_text": "In contrast to numerous other top-selling album artists, Springsteen continues to put out new music that tops the charts and fills stadiums, even in his late 60s, and he’s still performing at his peak. This comes as no surprise to me. He’s someone who narrates our most essential stories, and when you hear Born in the USA, those stories remain just as meaningful now as they always have been.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_13-b1_e2d7bfde", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_13-b1", "base_text": "Many of the major supermarket chains have come under fire with accusations of various unethical acts over the past decade. They've wasted tonnes of food, they've underpaid their suppliers and they've contributed to excessive plastic waste in their packaging, which has had its impact on our environment. But supermarkets and grocers are starting to sit up and take notice. In response to growing consumer backlash against the huge amounts of plastic waste generated by plastic packaging, some of the largest UK supermarkets have signed up to a pact promising to transform packaging and cut plastic wastage.", "probe_text": "Over the last ten years, several leading supermarket chains have faced criticism for a range of unethical behaviors. These include discarding large quantities of food, paying their suppliers insufficiently, and adding to the problem of plastic pollution through their packaging, all of which have affected the environment. However, supermarkets and grocery stores are beginning to pay attention. As a result of increasing public opposition to the significant plastic waste caused by packaging, a number of the biggest UK supermarkets have agreed to a pact aimed at overhauling packaging and reducing plastic waste.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_19-a2_f8275078", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_19-a2", "base_text": "More questions lie in looking at the influence of genetics on brain and language development. Are there genes that mutated and gave us language ability? Researchers have found a gene mutation that occurred between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which seems to have a connection with speaking and how our brains control our mouths and face. Monkeys have a similar gene, but it did not undergo this mutation. It's too early to say how much influence genes have on language, but one day the answers might be found in our DNA.", "probe_text": "Further inquiries focus on how genetics affect the development of the brain and language. Did certain gene mutations provide humans with the capacity for language? Scientists have identified a gene mutation that took place between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which appears linked to speech and the brain’s regulation of the mouth and facial muscles. Although monkeys possess a comparable gene, they did not experience this mutation. It remains uncertain how significantly genes impact language, but future discoveries in our DNA may reveal the answers.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_18-b1_b2fde903", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_18-b1", "base_text": "Unlike many other best-selling album artists, Springsteen is still releasing chart-topping, stadium-filling new music and remains at the top of his game in his late 60s. It doesn't surprise me at all. This is a man who tells us our most fundamental stories about ourselves and, when you listen to Born in the USA, those stories are as relevant today as they ever were.", "probe_text": "In contrast to numerous other top-selling album artists, Springsteen continues to put out new music that tops the charts and fills stadiums, maintaining his peak performance well into his late 60s. This comes as no shock to me. He is someone who narrates our deepest stories, and when you hear Born in the USA, those tales still hold just as much significance now as they always have.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_19-b1_b5274145", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_19-b1", "base_text": "More questions lie in looking at the influence of genetics on brain and language development. Are there genes that mutated and gave us language ability? Researchers have found a gene mutation that occurred between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which seems to have a connection with speaking and how our brains control our mouths and face. Monkeys have a similar gene, but it did not undergo this mutation. It's too early to say how much influence genes have on language, but one day the answers might be found in our DNA.", "probe_text": "Further inquiries focus on how genetics affect the development of the brain and language. Did certain gene mutations grant humans the capacity for language? Scientists have identified a gene mutation that took place between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which appears linked to speech and the brain’s regulation of the mouth and facial muscles. While monkeys possess a comparable gene, they did not experience this mutation. Although it is still uncertain to what extent genes impact language, future discoveries in our DNA may eventually provide answers.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_17-b1_ed7310e0", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_17-b1", "base_text": "Having an increased awareness of the possible differences in expectations and behaviour can help us avoid cases of miscommunication, but it is vital that we also remember that cultural stereotypes can be detrimental to building good business relationships. Although national cultures could play a part in shaping the way we behave and think, we are also largely influenced by the region we come from, the communities we associate with, our age and gender, our corporate culture and our individual experiences of the world. The knowledge of the potential differences should therefore be something we keep at the back of our minds, rather than something that we use to pigeonhole the individuals of an entire nation.", "probe_text": "Being more aware of potential differences in expectations and behaviors can help prevent misunderstandings, but it is equally important to recognize that relying on cultural stereotypes can harm the development of strong business relationships. While national culture may influence our thoughts and actions, we are also significantly shaped by our regional background, the communities we belong to, our age and gender, the culture of our workplace, and our unique personal experiences. Therefore, awareness of these possible differences should remain in our minds as a consideration, not as a way to categorize all individuals from a particular country.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_ASSET_test_0003_3cdab741", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0003", "base_text": "His next work, Saturday, follows an especially eventful day in the life of a successful neurosurgeon.", "probe_text": "His subsequent novel, Saturday, depicts a particularly significant day experienced by a prosperous neurosurgeon.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_TSAR_20-b1_cb99cb74", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "TSAR_20-b1", "base_text": "Julie Wong is a leading researcher in the field of Robotics and AI. She has been published extensively as author and co-author of over 50 papers in highly regarded, peer-reviewed journals. She frequently speaks at international conferences and was awarded the prestigious Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Julie lives with her family in a smart home she designed herself.", "probe_text": "Julie Wong is a prominent expert in Robotics and AI. She has authored or co-authored more than 50 articles in respected, peer-reviewed journals. She is a regular speaker at global conferences and received the esteemed Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Julie resides with her family in a smart home of her own design.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_ASSET_test_0006_0cae98d3", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0006", "base_text": "Since 2000, the recipient of the Kate Greenaway Medal has also been presented with the Colin Mears Award to the value of £5000.", "probe_text": "Beginning in 2000, the winner of the Kate Greenaway Medal has additionally received the Colin Mears Award, which is worth £5000.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_ASSET_test_0005_b2a2b419", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0005", "base_text": "They are culturally akin to the coastal peoples of Papua New Guinea.", "probe_text": "They share cultural similarities with the coastal communities of Papua New Guinea.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_ASSET_test_0007_ca0f9b67", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0007", "base_text": "Following the drummers are dancers, who often play the sogo (a tiny drum that makes almost no sound) and tend to have more elaborate — even acrobatic — choreography.", "probe_text": "Dancers come after the drummers, frequently playing the sogo (a small drum that produces very little noise) and usually performing more intricate, sometimes acrobatic, dance routines.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gpt_4_1_ASSET_test_0004_64db2821", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gpt-4.1", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0004", "base_text": "The tarantula, the trickster character, spun a black cord and, attaching it to the ball, crawled away fast to the east, pulling on the cord with all his strength.", "probe_text": "The trickster tarantula wove a black thread, fastened it to the ball, and quickly scurried eastward, tugging the thread with all his might.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
