{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_03-a2_55b73f57", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-a2", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "The Hunger Games serve as an annual reminder, orchestrated by the Capitol, of the districts' punishment for their past defiance. Annually, a lottery system selects one male and one female tribute between the ages of 12 and 18 from each district to engage in a mortal combat where only one participant can emerge victorious. These chosen adolescents are referred to as \"tributes\". The entire nation is compelled to witness this event via televised broadcast.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
