{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_06-a2_67313c1b", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_06-a2", "base_text": "One night in 2003, Suzanne Collins was flipping between channels on TV. It was at the time of the US invasion of Iraq. The only programmes she could find on TV were 'reality' programmes of young people competing to win a million dollars and news programmes about the war. Suzanne says that the two things start to merge together in a disturbing way and the idea for The Hunger Games came to her.", "probe_text": "In 2003, Suzanne Collins was channel surfing one evening when she stumbled upon an unsettling juxtaposition of television programming. As the US launched its invasion of Iraq, her viewing options were limited to reality TV shows featuring young contestants vying for a large cash prize and news coverage of the war. Collins recalls that these two disparate elements began to blur together in a disquieting manner, ultimately inspiring the concept for The Hunger Games.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
