{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_10-a2_1acac07d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_10-a2", "base_text": "I used to do a typical five-day week, but after I came out of my parental leave, I decided that I wanted to spend more time with my children before they start school. After negotiating with my boss, we decided to cut my working week down to a three-day work week. This of course meant a significant cut in my pay too, as I'm paid on a pro-rata basis. I've since noticed, though, that my workload hasn't decreased in the slightest! I'm now doing five days' worth of work in three days, but getting paid much less for it!", "probe_text": "Prior to taking parental leave, my schedule consisted of a standard Monday-to-Friday routine. However, upon returning to work, I made the conscious decision to prioritize family time before my children began their education. Following discussions with my employer, we agreed to reduce my working hours to a three-day week. As a result, my compensation was also reduced proportionally, given that my pay is calculated on a pro-rata basis. Notably, despite the decrease in workdays, I have observed no corresponding reduction in my workload; instead, I am now compressing five days' worth of tasks into a shorter timeframe while receiving significantly lower remuneration.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
