{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_12-a2_e8d929bb", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-a2", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "Around 50,000 to 100,000 years ago, signs of early human civilization begin to emerge, as seen in cave paintings, for instance; however, the link between these developments and the origin of language remains unclear. Although there was no abrupt increase in brain size, humans did exhibit increased complexity and intelligence. It is uncertain whether the use of language drove the evolution of their brains or if their growing cognitive abilities led to the emergence of language.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
