{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0030_235da628", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0030", "base_text": "Some towns on the Eyre Highway in the south-east corner of Western Australia, between the South Australian border almost as far as Caiguna, do not follow official Western Australian time.", "probe_text": "In the south-eastern region of Western Australia, specifically along the Eyre Highway near the South Australian border, towns such as those in the vicinity of Caiguna observe a non-standard time zone conforming to Western Australian regulations.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_01-a2_34b1bd95", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_01-a2", "base_text": "Now NASA is working towards logging some of the smaller asteroids, those measuring 140 metres wide or more. Of the 25,000 estimated asteroids of this size, so far about 8,000 have been logged, leaving 17,000 unaccounted for. Considering that a 19-metre asteroid that exploded above the city of Chelyabinsk in Russia in 2013 injured 1,200 people, these middle-sized asteroids would be a serious danger if they enter Earth's orbit.", "probe_text": "Currently, NASA is focusing efforts on cataloging the smaller asteroids – specifically, those with a diameter of 140 metres or greater. Approximately 8,000 of the roughly 25,000 asteroids believed to be this size have been identified, meaning around 17,000 remain undiscovered. Given that an asteroid only 19 metres in size caused injuries to 1,200 individuals when it exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, in 2013, these mid-sized asteroids represent a significant threat should they cross Earth’s path.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
