{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_03-b1_3e19c11c", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-b1", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "As a yearly occurrence, the Capitol stages the Hunger Games to serve as a constant reminder of the districts’ failed uprising and subsequent penalty. A boy and a girl between the ages of twelve and eighteen are randomly selected from each district to fight to the death, with only a single victor permitted. These selected youths are known as “tributes,” and the entire country is compelled to view the spectacle on television.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
