{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_17-a2_42a501bf", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_17-a2", "base_text": "Having an increased awareness of the possible differences in expectations and behaviour can help us avoid cases of miscommunication, but it is vital that we also remember that cultural stereotypes can be detrimental to building good business relationships. Although national cultures could play a part in shaping the way we behave and think, we are also largely influenced by the region we come from, the communities we associate with, our age and gender, our corporate culture and our individual experiences of the world. The knowledge of the potential differences should therefore be something we keep at the back of our minds, rather than something that we use to pigeonhole the individuals of an entire nation.", "probe_text": "Being mindful of the potential variations in expectations and behavior can aid in preventing miscommunication, but it is equally crucial to recognize that relying on cultural stereotypes can hinder the development of successful business relationships. While national cultures may contribute to our behavioral patterns and thought processes, regional backgrounds, social affiliations, age, gender, organizational culture, and personal life experiences also significantly shape who we are. Consequently, an understanding of these potential differences should serve as a subtle consideration, rather than a rigid framework for categorizing individuals based solely on their nationality.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
