{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_11-b1_680970dc", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_11-b1", "base_text": "Much of today's business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture. A lack of understanding of the cultural norms and practices of our business acquaintances can result in unfair judgements, misunderstandings and breakdowns in communication. Here are three basic areas of differences in the business etiquette around the world that could help stand you in good stead when you next find yourself working with someone from a different culture.", "probe_text": "A significant amount of commercial activity now takes place internationally, and although English is frequently used as a shared language within the global business world, the subtle details and anticipated behaviors of professional communication can vary considerably between cultures. Failing to appreciate the customary norms and practices of international colleagues may lead to inaccurate assessments, confusion, and communication failures. The following three key areas of differing business customs can be beneficial when collaborating with individuals from diverse cultural backgrounds.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0133_f2d271d1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0133", "base_text": "He came from a musical family; his mother, LaRue, was an administrative assistant and singer, and his father, Keith Brion, was a band director at Yale.", "probe_text": "Coming from a household steeped in music, his mother, LaRue, worked as both an administrator and vocalist, while his father, Keith Brion, held the position of band conductor at Yale.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0134_33999ef4", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0134", "base_text": "The largest populations of Mennonites are in Canada, Democratic Republic of Congo and the United States, but Mennonites can also be found in tight-knit communities in at least 51 countries on six continents or scattered amongst the populace of those countries.", "probe_text": "Mennonite communities are predominantly located in Canada, the Democratic Republic of Congo, and the United States, yet they can also be discovered in close-knit settlements across a minimum of 51 nations spanning six continents, as well as dispersed throughout the general population of those countries.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0135_c8292115", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0135", "base_text": "Naas is a major \"Dublin Suburb\" town, with many people living in Naas and working in Dublin.", "probe_text": "Naas serves as a significant commuter hub, boasting a substantial population that commutes daily to the capital city of Dublin for employment.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0136_85f57c4f", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0136", "base_text": "Acanthopholis's armour consisted of oval plates set almost horizontally into the skin, with spikes protruding from the neck and shoulder area, along the spine.", "probe_text": "Acanthopholis's armor was comprised of elliptical scutes embedded nearly flat in its dermal layer, featuring pointed projections arising from the cervical and scapular regions, as well as along its vertebral column.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_12-a2_3bb81555", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-a2", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "However, evidence of the beginnings of human culture appears from around 100,000 to 50,000 years in the past—cave art is one instance—but the link to the development of language remains a mystery. While brain size didn’t increase rapidly, humans nonetheless became increasingly sophisticated and clever. It’s unclear whether the emergence of language spurred brain growth, or if more advanced brains were the origin of language itself.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_70b_TSAR_19-a2_ad009d2d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-70b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_19-a2", "base_text": "More questions lie in looking at the influence of genetics on brain and language development. Are there genes that mutated and gave us language ability? Researchers have found a gene mutation that occurred between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which seems to have a connection with speaking and how our brains control our mouths and face. Monkeys have a similar gene, but it did not undergo this mutation. It's too early to say how much influence genes have on language, but one day the answers might be found in our DNA.", "probe_text": "Further inquiry is warranted into the role of genetics in shaping brain and language development. Did specific genetic mutations confer upon us the capacity for language? Scientists have identified a gene mutation that took place between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which appears to be linked to speech production and the neural mechanisms governing facial and oral motor control. A comparable gene is present in monkeys, yet it did not undergo this particular mutation. While the extent of genetic influence on language remains uncertain, future discoveries may ultimately reside within our genetic code.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0137_2ecba915", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0137", "base_text": "Origin Irmo was chartered on Christmas Eve in 1890 in response to the opening of the Columbia, Newberry and Laurens Railroad.", "probe_text": "Incorporated in 1890 on a Christmas evening, Irmo's establishment was directly linked to the launch of the Columbia, Newberry and Laurens Railroad.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0139_c3e58582", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0139", "base_text": "In the years before his final release in 1474, when he began preparations for the reconquest of Wallachia, Vlad resided with his new wife in a house in the Hungarian capital.", "probe_text": "Prior to his liberation from captivity in 1474, while making arrangements for the recapture of Wallachia, Vlad lived with his newlywed spouse in a residence within Hungary's central city.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0141_78c730fe", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0141", "base_text": "Bone marrow is the flexible tissue found in the hollow interior of bones.", "probe_text": "The spongy substance within the cavities of bones is known as bone marrow.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0142_6cbf4fdf", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0142", "base_text": "Reflection nebulae are usually blue because the scattering is more efficient for blue light than red (this is the same scattering process that gives us blue skies and red sunsets).", "probe_text": "Blue reflection nebulae owe their color to a phenomenon where shorter wavelengths of light, such as those in the blue spectrum, are dispersed more effectively than longer wavelengths like red, a process identical to what occurs during the formation of blue skies and reddened sunsets.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_12-b1_5c9cd200", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-b1", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "However, evidence of the beginnings of human culture appears from around 50,000 to 100,000 years in the past—cave art is one example—but the link to the development of language remains a mystery. While brain size didn’t increase rapidly, humans nonetheless became increasingly sophisticated and clever. It’s unclear whether language use drove brain evolution, or if more advanced brains were the origin of language itself.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_llama_3_1_8b_ASSET_test_0144_12795703", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "llama-3.1-8b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0144", "base_text": "MacGruber starts asking for simple objects to make something to defuse the bomb, but he is later distracted by something (usually involving his personal life) that makes him run out of time.", "probe_text": "MacGruber begins requesting basic items in an attempt to disarm the explosive device, only to be sidetracked by a personal matter at some point, ultimately leaving him with insufficient time.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
