{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_TSAR_14-a2_96d3816d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_14-a2", "base_text": "Wasfia Nazreen first came across a hula hoop as a young girl, when she saw a foreign child who was visiting her native Bangladesh playing with one. Wasfia reluctantly stood by and watched, as in her country it was believed that girls should not play with hula hoops or ride bikes. Now Wasfia is one of the few people in the world to have climbed the Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and the first to have hula-hooped on each peak. Her reason for doing so: to empower women and girls in a country which discourages them from doing sport.", "probe_text": "As a child, Wasfia Nazreen’s initial encounter with a hula hoop occurred when a visiting child from another country played with one while in her homeland of Bangladesh. Wasfia observed with hesitation, because cultural norms in her nation dictated that girls were not permitted to engage in activities like hula-hooping or cycling. Today, Wasfia is a rare individual globally who has summited the Seven Summits – encompassing mountains like Everest and Kilimanjaro – and uniquely, she has hula-hooped atop each one. Her motivation for this achievement is to encourage and uplift women and girls in a nation where participation in sports is often discouraged for them.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
