{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_ASSET_val_1990_3c93f239", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1990", "base_text": "It was launched on 7 December 1998, and is available on digital television via satellite, cable, IPTV and terrestrial (Freeview) platforms.", "probe_text": "The service began broadcasting on December 7, 1998, and can be accessed through various digital television methods including satellite, cable, IPTV, and terrestrial (Freeview) services.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_ASSET_val_1991_3b889775", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1991", "base_text": "In October 1983, the Grameen Bank Project was transformed into an independent bank by government legislation.", "probe_text": "Through governmental lawmaking in October of 1983, the Grameen Bank Project became a self-governing, standalone bank.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_ASSET_val_1992_bee205e8", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1992", "base_text": "In practice, however, the definition means that high-precision realizations of the second should compensate for the effects of the ambient temperature (black-body radiation) within which atomic clocks operate, and extrapolate accordingly to the value of the second at a temperature of absolute zero.", "probe_text": "Actually, this definition requires that highly accurate measurements of the second account for the influence of surrounding temperature – specifically, black-body radiation – on the functioning of atomic clocks, and then calculate the second’s value as if the temperature were absolute zero.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_gemma_3_27b_ASSET_val_1994_63f28deb", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "gemma-3-27b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_val_1994", "base_text": "A console manufacturer is a company that manufactures and distributes video game consoles.", "probe_text": "Companies that create and sell video game consoles are known as console manufacturers.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_03-b1_649c56da", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-b1", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "The Capitol hosts the annual Hunger Games as a reminder to the districts of their rebellion's consequences. Each year, through a lottery, one male and one female between the ages of 12 and 18 from every district are selected to participate in a fight where only one can emerge alive. These selected youths are referred to as \"tributes.\" The entire country is compelled to observe this event on television.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_03-a2_9f1e0ccd", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_03-a2", "base_text": "The Hunger Games are an annual event, which the Capitol uses to remind the districts that they are being punished for rebelling. Every year one boy and one girl aged 12 to 18 are chosen from each district by lottery to compete in a battle in which only one person can survive. The chosen teenagers are called \"tributes\". The whole nation is forced to watch the event on television.", "probe_text": "Each year, the Capitol hosts the Hunger Games as a reminder to the districts of their rebellion's consequences. Through a lottery system, one male and one female between the ages of 12 and 18 from each district are selected to participate in a fight where only one can emerge alive. These selected youths are referred to as \"tributes.\" The entire country is compelled to view this event on television.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_02-b1_accaa3bd", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_02-b1", "base_text": "Earthquakes damage all structures, including bridges. Luckily, this kind of collapse is relatively infrequent, especially with modern bridges. Engineers have learned to design bridges in earthquake zones on areas that are much more resistant to movement.", "probe_text": "Earthquakes can destroy various constructions, such as bridges. Thankfully, this type of structural failure doesn't happen very often, particularly with contemporary bridge designs. Experts now create plans for bridges in seismic regions that significantly reduce the risk of shaking-induced damage.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_01-b1_fe463fbe", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_01-b1", "base_text": "Now NASA is working towards logging some of the smaller asteroids, those measuring 140 metres wide or more. Of the 25,000 estimated asteroids of this size, so far about 8,000 have been logged, leaving 17,000 unaccounted for. Considering that a 19-metre asteroid that exploded above the city of Chelyabinsk in Russia in 2013 injured 1,200 people, these middle-sized asteroids would be a serious danger if they enter Earth's orbit.", "probe_text": "Currently, NASA is focused on cataloging asteroids that are at least 140 meters in diameter. Out of the approximately 25,000 such asteroids, around 8,000 have been recorded, with 17,000 still unidentified. Given that a 19-meter asteroid exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, in 2013 and injured 1,200 individuals, these medium-sized space rocks could pose a significant threat if they were to approach Earth's orbit.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_04-a2_a0c7e263", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_04-a2", "base_text": "Small animals like birds, squirrels, mice and insects are a common sight in many cities and towns. But in recent years, the world's press has been full of reports of wild animals coming into cities in bigger and bigger numbers. There have been bears in Vancouver parks, leopards on the streets of Mumbai and wild pigs in gardens in Berlin. What happens when bigger animals come into our cities? Are they welcome or are they considered a danger or a pest?", "probe_text": "In numerous urban areas, creatures such as birds, squirrels, mice, and insects frequently appear. However, there has been an increasing trend of larger wild animals appearing in city environments over the past few years. Incidents include bears spotted in Vancouver’s parks, leopards wandering through Mumbai's streets, and wild boars found in Berlin gardens. The question arises: do these larger animals receive a warm welcome in cities, or are they viewed as threats or nuisances?", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_02-a2_6507e5cd", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_02-a2", "base_text": "Earthquakes damage all structures, including bridges. Luckily, this kind of collapse is relatively infrequent, especially with modern bridges. Engineers have learned to design bridges in earthquake zones on areas that are much more resistant to movement.", "probe_text": "Earthquakes can destroy various constructions, such as bridges. Fortunately, this type of failure occurs rarely, particularly for contemporary bridges. Designers now create structures in seismic regions that are significantly better at withstanding tremors.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_04-b1_800e7dde", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_04-b1", "base_text": "Small animals like birds, squirrels, mice and insects are a common sight in many cities and towns. But in recent years, the world's press has been full of reports of wild animals coming into cities in bigger and bigger numbers. There have been bears in Vancouver parks, leopards on the streets of Mumbai and wild pigs in gardens in Berlin. What happens when bigger animals come into our cities? Are they welcome or are they considered a danger or a pest?", "probe_text": "In numerous urban areas, one frequently spots small creatures such as birds, squirrels, mice, and insects. However, there has been an increasing trend of larger wild animals appearing in city environments, as reported by global media. Examples include bears strolling through Vancouver parks, leopards wandering the streets of Mumbai, and wild boars roaming Berlin gardens. The question arises: do these larger animals receive a warm welcome in cities, or are they viewed as threats or nuisances?", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_01-a2_950a2c95", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_01-a2", "base_text": "Now NASA is working towards logging some of the smaller asteroids, those measuring 140 metres wide or more. Of the 25,000 estimated asteroids of this size, so far about 8,000 have been logged, leaving 17,000 unaccounted for. Considering that a 19-metre asteroid that exploded above the city of Chelyabinsk in Russia in 2013 injured 1,200 people, these middle-sized asteroids would be a serious danger if they enter Earth's orbit.", "probe_text": "Currently, NASA is focused on cataloging asteroids that are at least 140 meters in diameter. Out of an estimated 25,000 such asteroids, approximately 8,000 have been recorded, with around 17,000 yet to be identified. Given that a 19-meter asteroid exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, in 2013 and injured 1,200 individuals, these medium-sized asteroids could pose a significant threat if they were to approach Earth's orbit.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_05-a2_be4639fa", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_05-a2", "base_text": "Potentially Hazardous Asteroids' are rocks close enough to pass within 7.5 million kilometres of Earth's orbit. NASA has created a map of 1,400 PHAs, none of which are expected to be a threat in the next one hundred years. With technology already available, NASA can track these objects and make predictions about possible impact, at which point two defence solutions could be launched.", "probe_text": "\"Potentially Hazardous Asteroids\" are space rocks that come within 7.5 million kilometers of Earth’s orbital path. NASA has mapped out approximately 1,400 PHAs, none of which pose a threat to our planet over the next century. Using current technology, NASA is capable of monitoring these objects and forecasting any potential collisions. Should an impact be predicted, two defensive measures could be deployed.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_05-b1_d8e980bc", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_05-b1", "base_text": "Potentially Hazardous Asteroids' are rocks close enough to pass within 7.5 million kilometres of Earth's orbit. NASA has created a map of 1,400 PHAs, none of which are expected to be a threat in the next one hundred years. With technology already available, NASA can track these objects and make predictions about possible impact, at which point two defence solutions could be launched.", "probe_text": "\"Potentially Hazardous Asteroids\" are space rocks that come within 7.5 million kilometers of Earth’s orbital path. NASA has mapped approximately 1,400 PHAs, and none are anticipated to pose a danger over the next century. Using current technology, NASA is capable of monitoring these objects and forecasting potential collisions, after which two defensive measures could be deployed.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_06-a2_7561e660", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_06-a2", "base_text": "One night in 2003, Suzanne Collins was flipping between channels on TV. It was at the time of the US invasion of Iraq. The only programmes she could find on TV were 'reality' programmes of young people competing to win a million dollars and news programmes about the war. Suzanne says that the two things start to merge together in a disturbing way and the idea for The Hunger Games came to her.", "probe_text": "In 2003, during the US invasion of Iraq, Suzanne Collins was channel surfing one evening. She discovered only reality shows featuring young contestants vying for a million-dollar prize and news coverage of the conflict. According to Collins, these two types of programming began blending in an unsettling manner, inspiring her concept for The Hunger Games.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_06-b1_7dcf07c1", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_06-b1", "base_text": "One night in 2003, Suzanne Collins was flipping between channels on TV. It was at the time of the US invasion of Iraq. The only programmes she could find on TV were 'reality' programmes of young people competing to win a million dollars and news programmes about the war. Suzanne says that the two things start to merge together in a disturbing way and the idea for The Hunger Games came to her.", "probe_text": "In 2003, during the US invasion of Iraq, Suzanne Collins was channel surfing one evening. She only encountered 'reality' shows featuring young contestants vying for a million dollars and news segments about the conflict. Collins noted that these two types of programming began to blend in an unsettling manner, sparking the concept for The Hunger Games.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_07-a2_0d718b69", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_07-a2", "base_text": "The Life of Pi tells the extraordinary story of Pi, a teenage boy from India, who is shipwrecked and finds himself trapped in a lifeboat in the Pacific Ocean with a ferocious tiger. It is the third book by the Canadian author Yann Martel, and was published in 2001. Since then, it has sold over seven million copies worldwide, won several prizes and been translated into 41 languages. Martel is the son of a diplomat and as a child lived in Costa Rica, Canada, France and Mexico.", "probe_text": "Yann Martel's third book, \"The Life of Pi,\" narrates an incredible tale about Pi, an Indian teenager who becomes stranded at sea on a lifeboat with a tiger after a shipwreck in the Pacific Ocean. This novel, released in 2001, has achieved remarkable success, selling more than seven million copies globally and earning multiple awards while being translated into 41 languages. The Canadian author, whose father was a diplomat, spent his childhood years in Costa Rica, Canada, France, and Mexico.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_07-b1_87c39d17", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_07-b1", "base_text": "The Life of Pi tells the extraordinary story of Pi, a teenage boy from India, who is shipwrecked and finds himself trapped in a lifeboat in the Pacific Ocean with a ferocious tiger. It is the third book by the Canadian author Yann Martel, and was published in 2001. Since then, it has sold over seven million copies worldwide, won several prizes and been translated into 41 languages. Martel is the son of a diplomat and as a child lived in Costa Rica, Canada, France and Mexico.", "probe_text": "Yann Martel's third book, \"The Life of Pi,\" narrates an exceptional tale about Pi, an Indian teenager who becomes stranded at sea with a deadly tiger after a shipwreck in the Pacific Ocean. This novel, released in 2001, has achieved remarkable success, selling more than seven million copies globally and earning numerous awards while being translated into forty-one languages. The Canadian writer, whose father was a diplomat, spent his childhood years in Costa Rica, Canada, France, and Mexico.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_08-a2_0d1edf58", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_08-a2", "base_text": "Dreams of a phone call from an old friend or the death of someone close, for example, are more likely to be the result of coincidence than prophecy. And, of course, we probably choose to forget all the times we dream about such events but they don't happen.", "probe_text": "Fantasies of receiving a call from a long-lost companion or envisioning the demise of a beloved individual are typically coincidental rather than prophetic. Additionally, it’s likely that we overlook instances where we dream of these occurrences and they never come to pass.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_08-b1_f76a843e", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_08-b1", "base_text": "Dreams of a phone call from an old friend or the death of someone close, for example, are more likely to be the result of coincidence than prophecy. And, of course, we probably choose to forget all the times we dream about such events but they don't happen.", "probe_text": "Fantasies of receiving a call from a long-lost companion or envisioning the demise of a dear one are often coincidental rather than prophetic. Additionally, it's likely that we overlook instances where we dream of these occurrences, yet they never come to pass.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_09-a2_80bb28b8", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_09-a2", "base_text": "Some bridge collapses are mysteries, and engineers only realise why after they conduct a complete investigation. In some cases, this could happen because inferior-quality material was used in the construction, or because of a defect in a key piece of the bridge. In other cases, the bridge was designed only to support a certain amount of weight and no more.", "probe_text": "Certain bridge failures remain enigmatic until engineers thoroughly investigate them. Such collapses might be due to substandard materials used during construction or a flaw in an essential component of the bridge. Alternatively, the design may have been intended to bear only a specific load limit and nothing beyond that.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_09-b1_e39b86f4", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_09-b1", "base_text": "Some bridge collapses are mysteries, and engineers only realise why after they conduct a complete investigation. In some cases, this could happen because inferior-quality material was used in the construction, or because of a defect in a key piece of the bridge. In other cases, the bridge was designed only to support a certain amount of weight and no more.", "probe_text": "Certain bridge failures remain enigmatic until engineers carry out thorough examinations. These incidents might be due to substandard materials used during building or a flaw in an essential component. Alternatively, the design may have been intended to bear only a specific load limit.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_10-a2_7b9fcd20", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_10-a2", "base_text": "I used to do a typical five-day week, but after I came out of my parental leave, I decided that I wanted to spend more time with my children before they start school. After negotiating with my boss, we decided to cut my working week down to a three-day work week. This of course meant a significant cut in my pay too, as I'm paid on a pro-rata basis. I've since noticed, though, that my workload hasn't decreased in the slightest! I'm now doing five days' worth of work in three days, but getting paid much less for it!", "probe_text": "Previously, I followed a standard five-day work schedule, but upon returning from parental leave, I chose to prioritize spending more time with my kids before they begin school. After discussions with my supervisor, we agreed to reduce my workweek to three days. Consequently, my salary was also significantly reduced since I am compensated on a pro-rata basis. However, I've observed that the amount of work hasn't lessened at all; now, I complete five days’ worth of tasks within three days while earning considerably less.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_10-b1_15c8c33d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_10-b1", "base_text": "I used to do a typical five-day week, but after I came out of my parental leave, I decided that I wanted to spend more time with my children before they start school. After negotiating with my boss, we decided to cut my working week down to a three-day work week. This of course meant a significant cut in my pay too, as I'm paid on a pro-rata basis. I've since noticed, though, that my workload hasn't decreased in the slightest! I'm now doing five days' worth of work in three days, but getting paid much less for it!", "probe_text": "Previously, I followed a standard five-day work schedule. However, following my return from parental leave, I chose to prioritize spending more time with my kids before they begin school. After discussions with my supervisor, we agreed to reduce my workweek to three days. Consequently, my salary was also significantly reduced since I am compensated on a pro-rata basis. Interestingly, despite the shorter week, my workload remains unchanged. Now, I complete five days of tasks within three days but receive considerably less pay for it.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_11-a2_453c66a4", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_11-a2", "base_text": "Much of today's business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture. A lack of understanding of the cultural norms and practices of our business acquaintances can result in unfair judgements, misunderstandings and breakdowns in communication. Here are three basic areas of differences in the business etiquette around the world that could help stand you in good stead when you next find yourself working with someone from a different culture.", "probe_text": "A significant portion of current commerce transcends national boundaries, and although English often serves as a universal language for global trade, the subtleties and standards of business interaction can vary widely between cultures. Failing to grasp the cultural customs and behaviors of our professional contacts may lead to misinterpretations, conflicts, and communication failures. Understanding these three fundamental aspects of international business etiquette could prove beneficial when collaborating with individuals from diverse backgrounds.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_11-b1_40a0eb99", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_11-b1", "base_text": "Much of today's business is conducted across international borders, and while the majority of the global business community might share the use of English as a common language, the nuances and expectations of business communication might differ greatly from culture to culture. A lack of understanding of the cultural norms and practices of our business acquaintances can result in unfair judgements, misunderstandings and breakdowns in communication. Here are three basic areas of differences in the business etiquette around the world that could help stand you in good stead when you next find yourself working with someone from a different culture.", "probe_text": "A significant portion of current commerce occurs across international lines, and although English often serves as the universal language for global trade, the subtleties and anticipations within business interactions can vary widely between cultures. Failing to grasp the cultural standards and behaviors of our professional contacts may lead to misinterpretations, conflicts, and communication failures. Understanding these three fundamental aspects of worldwide business etiquette differences could greatly benefit you when collaborating with individuals from diverse cultural backgrounds.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_12-a2_46a4171a", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-a2", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "From around 100,000 to 50,000 years ago, signs of early human civilization begin to appear, such as cave paintings; however, the link to language remains unknown. Despite no sudden increase in brain size, humans did grow more sophisticated and smarter. Did the development of language lead to further brain evolution? Or did increasingly complex brains give rise to language first?", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_12-b1_75ff75a5", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_12-b1", "base_text": "Between 100,000 and 50,000 years ago, though, we start to see the evidence of early human civilisation, through cave paintings for example; no one knows the connection between this and language. Brains didn't suddenly get bigger, yet humans did become more complex and more intelligent. Was it using language that caused their brains to develop? Or did their more complex brains start producing language?", "probe_text": "From around 100,000 to 50,000 years ago, signs of early human civilization begin to appear, such as cave paintings; however, the link to language remains unknown. Despite no sudden increase in brain size, humans did grow more sophisticated and smarter. Did the development of language lead to changes in their brains? Or did more intricate brains give rise to language first?", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_13-a2_575f88aa", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_13-a2", "base_text": "Many of the major supermarket chains have come under fire with accusations of various unethical acts over the past decade. They've wasted tonnes of food, they've underpaid their suppliers and they've contributed to excessive plastic waste in their packaging, which has had its impact on our environment. But supermarkets and grocers are starting to sit up and take notice. In response to growing consumer backlash against the huge amounts of plastic waste generated by plastic packaging, some of the largest UK supermarkets have signed up to a pact promising to transform packaging and cut plastic wastage.", "probe_text": "Over the last ten years, numerous significant supermarket corporations have faced criticism for engaging in multiple unethical practices. These companies have been accused of discarding large quantities of food, paying their suppliers less than fair prices, and generating considerable environmental harm through excessive use of plastic packaging. However, these businesses are beginning to acknowledge the issues. As a reaction to increasing customer disapproval regarding the substantial plastic waste produced by packaging, several major UK supermarkets have committed to an agreement aimed at altering their packaging methods and reducing plastic waste.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_13-b1_960c3abb", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_13-b1", "base_text": "Many of the major supermarket chains have come under fire with accusations of various unethical acts over the past decade. They've wasted tonnes of food, they've underpaid their suppliers and they've contributed to excessive plastic waste in their packaging, which has had its impact on our environment. But supermarkets and grocers are starting to sit up and take notice. In response to growing consumer backlash against the huge amounts of plastic waste generated by plastic packaging, some of the largest UK supermarkets have signed up to a pact promising to transform packaging and cut plastic wastage.", "probe_text": "Over the last ten years, numerous significant supermarket corporations have faced criticism for engaging in multiple unethical practices. These include squandering vast quantities of food, paying their suppliers less than fair prices, and producing an overwhelming amount of plastic waste through their packaging, which has negatively affected our environment. However, these businesses are beginning to acknowledge the issues. As a reaction to increasing consumer disapproval regarding excessive plastic waste from packaging, several major UK supermarkets have committed to an agreement aimed at altering their packaging methods and reducing plastic waste.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_14-a2_91a6c191", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_14-a2", "base_text": "Wasfia Nazreen first came across a hula hoop as a young girl, when she saw a foreign child who was visiting her native Bangladesh playing with one. Wasfia reluctantly stood by and watched, as in her country it was believed that girls should not play with hula hoops or ride bikes. Now Wasfia is one of the few people in the world to have climbed the Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and the first to have hula-hooped on each peak. Her reason for doing so: to empower women and girls in a country which discourages them from doing sport.", "probe_text": "As a young girl in Bangladesh, Wasfia Nazreen encountered a hula hoop for the first time while observing a visiting foreign child playing with one. She hesitantly observed the scene since her culture held that girls shouldn’t engage in activities like hula hooping or cycling. Today, Wasfia has become one of the rare individuals to conquer all Seven Summits, such as Everest and Kilimanjaro, and she is distinguished for being the first person to hula hoop on each summit. Her objective is to inspire women and girls in a society that often discourages them from participating in sports.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_14-b1_624aacb3", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_14-b1", "base_text": "Wasfia Nazreen first came across a hula hoop as a young girl, when she saw a foreign child who was visiting her native Bangladesh playing with one. Wasfia reluctantly stood by and watched, as in her country it was believed that girls should not play with hula hoops or ride bikes. Now Wasfia is one of the few people in the world to have climbed the Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and the first to have hula-hooped on each peak. Her reason for doing so: to empower women and girls in a country which discourages them from doing sport.", "probe_text": "As a young girl in Bangladesh, Wasfia Nazreen encountered a hula hoop for the first time when she observed a visiting foreign child playing with one. She watched hesitantly because in her culture, it was thought inappropriate for girls to engage in such activities or even ride bicycles. Today, Wasfia stands out as one of the rare individuals who have conquered all Seven Summits, including Everest and Kilimanjaro, and she is distinguished further by being the first person to hula hoop on each summit. Her goal is to inspire women and girls in a society where participating in sports is discouraged for them.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_15-a2_b385ba52", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_15-a2", "base_text": "On the basis of these findings, we recommend that Girox Foods adopt a more people-oriented management style. As part of this, we should explore ways of offering more flexible working hours to our employees and consider how we can enable employees to work from their chosen locations. Managers need to consider a variety of ways to challenge our staff and provide more positive reinforcement and recognition of the work they do. Increased investment in professional development, especially in the area of social and intercultural communication, could also contribute to creating a positive environment for employees to produce their best work.", "probe_text": "Based on these results, Girox Foods should implement a more employee-centered leadership approach. This includes investigating options for flexible scheduling and assessing methods that allow staff to work from preferred locations. It is essential for supervisors to think of various strategies to motivate employees and acknowledge their efforts more positively. Additionally, enhancing professional growth opportunities, particularly in social and intercultural communication skills, can help foster a supportive workplace where employees are motivated to excel.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_15-b1_dc36b3c5", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_15-b1", "base_text": "On the basis of these findings, we recommend that Girox Foods adopt a more people-oriented management style. As part of this, we should explore ways of offering more flexible working hours to our employees and consider how we can enable employees to work from their chosen locations. Managers need to consider a variety of ways to challenge our staff and provide more positive reinforcement and recognition of the work they do. Increased investment in professional development, especially in the area of social and intercultural communication, could also contribute to creating a positive environment for employees to produce their best work.", "probe_text": "Based on these results, we advise Girox Foods to implement a more employee-centered leadership approach. This includes investigating options for flexible scheduling and assessing methods that allow staff to operate from preferred locations. Supervisors should look into diverse strategies for motivating employees and acknowledging their efforts effectively. Additionally, boosting resources for career advancement, particularly in social and intercultural interaction skills, may help foster an environment where workers can excel.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_16-a2_f4b01b54", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_16-a2", "base_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "probe_text": "Over the past two hundred years, advancements in technology and health care have led to a significant decrease in child mortality, spurring a boom in population growth. As a result, these expanded families continued to grow with more surviving children reaching adulthood and starting their own families. However, following the increased access to contraception in the 1960s, the global average fertility rate has dropped from six offspring per woman down to as few as two.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_16-b1_a3c6fb6e", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_16-b1", "base_text": "In the last two centuries, improvements in technology and health meant fewer children died young, fuelling rapid population growth. These large families produced even more children who survived into adulthood and had their own children. But with the wider availability of contraception in the 1960s, the global average number of babies per woman has declined from six babies per woman to as low as two.", "probe_text": "Over the past two hundred years, advancements in technology and health care have led to a significant decrease in child mortality, driving swift population expansion. This trend resulted in larger families with more children reaching adulthood and starting their own families. However, following the increased access to contraception in the 1960s, the global average fertility rate has dropped from six offspring per woman to as few as two.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_17-a2_48f9dcdc", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_17-a2", "base_text": "Having an increased awareness of the possible differences in expectations and behaviour can help us avoid cases of miscommunication, but it is vital that we also remember that cultural stereotypes can be detrimental to building good business relationships. Although national cultures could play a part in shaping the way we behave and think, we are also largely influenced by the region we come from, the communities we associate with, our age and gender, our corporate culture and our individual experiences of the world. The knowledge of the potential differences should therefore be something we keep at the back of our minds, rather than something that we use to pigeonhole the individuals of an entire nation.", "probe_text": "Being more mindful of potential variations in expectations and behavior can assist us in preventing miscommunication, but it is crucial to also acknowledge that cultural stereotypes can harm the development of strong business ties. While national cultures may influence our actions and thoughts, we are equally shaped by factors such as regional background, community affiliations, age, gender, corporate environment, and personal world experiences. Thus, awareness of these differences should serve as a subtle guide rather than a tool for categorizing an entire nation's individuals.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_17-b1_c2141868", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_17-b1", "base_text": "Having an increased awareness of the possible differences in expectations and behaviour can help us avoid cases of miscommunication, but it is vital that we also remember that cultural stereotypes can be detrimental to building good business relationships. Although national cultures could play a part in shaping the way we behave and think, we are also largely influenced by the region we come from, the communities we associate with, our age and gender, our corporate culture and our individual experiences of the world. The knowledge of the potential differences should therefore be something we keep at the back of our minds, rather than something that we use to pigeonhole the individuals of an entire nation.", "probe_text": "Being more conscious of potential variations in expectations and behavior can assist us in preventing misunderstandings; however, it is crucial to bear in mind that cultural stereotypes can harm the development of solid business connections. While national cultures may influence our actions and thoughts, regional backgrounds, community affiliations, age, gender, corporate culture, and personal world experiences also significantly impact us. Therefore, awareness of these differences should serve as a subtle guide rather than a basis for categorizing an entire nation's individuals.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_18-a2_b556ed34", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_18-a2", "base_text": "Unlike many other best-selling album artists, Springsteen is still releasing chart-topping, stadium-filling new music and remains at the top of his game in his late 60s. It doesn't surprise me at all. This is a man who tells us our most fundamental stories about ourselves and, when you listen to Born in the USA, those stories are as relevant today as they ever were.", "probe_text": "In contrast to numerous top-selling album performers, Springsteen continues to produce hit music that fills stadiums and maintains his excellence into his late 60s. This fact doesn't astonish me in the least. He is an artist who narrates our core personal narratives, and albums like Born in the USA showcase stories that remain just as pertinent now as they were originally.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_18-b1_50cc310d", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_18-b1", "base_text": "Unlike many other best-selling album artists, Springsteen is still releasing chart-topping, stadium-filling new music and remains at the top of his game in his late 60s. It doesn't surprise me at all. This is a man who tells us our most fundamental stories about ourselves and, when you listen to Born in the USA, those stories are as relevant today as they ever were.", "probe_text": "In contrast to numerous top-selling album performers, Springsteen continues to produce hit songs that pack arenas and maintains his peak performance well into his late 60s. This fact doesn’t astonish me in the least. He is an artist who narrates our core personal narratives, and albums like Born in the USA showcase stories that remain just as pertinent now as they were originally.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 2, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_19-a2_3c1db672", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_19-a2", "base_text": "More questions lie in looking at the influence of genetics on brain and language development. Are there genes that mutated and gave us language ability? Researchers have found a gene mutation that occurred between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which seems to have a connection with speaking and how our brains control our mouths and face. Monkeys have a similar gene, but it did not undergo this mutation. It's too early to say how much influence genes have on language, but one day the answers might be found in our DNA.", "probe_text": "Exploring the impact of genetics on brain and language growth raises numerous questions. Did genetic alterations provide humans with their capacity for speech? Scientists discovered a gene alteration that took place between 200,000 to 100,000 years ago, which appears linked to vocal control and how our brains manage mouth and facial movements. While monkeys possess a comparable gene, it has not experienced this change. The extent of genetics' role in language remains uncertain; however, future insights could emerge from studying DNA.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_19-b1_fcda8fdf", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_19-b1", "base_text": "More questions lie in looking at the influence of genetics on brain and language development. Are there genes that mutated and gave us language ability? Researchers have found a gene mutation that occurred between 200,000 and 100,000 years ago, which seems to have a connection with speaking and how our brains control our mouths and face. Monkeys have a similar gene, but it did not undergo this mutation. It's too early to say how much influence genes have on language, but one day the answers might be found in our DNA.", "probe_text": "Investigating the impact of genetics on brain and language growth raises further questions. Did mutations in certain genes grant us the capacity for speech? Scientists discovered a genetic alteration that took place between 200,000 to 100,000 years ago, seemingly linked to our ability to speak and how our brains manage mouth and facial movements. While monkeys possess a comparable gene, it has not experienced this change. The extent of genetics' role in language remains uncertain, but future insights could come from analyzing our DNA.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_20-a2_f737f735", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_20-a2", "base_text": "Julie Wong is a leading researcher in the field of Robotics and AI. She has been published extensively as author and co-author of over 50 papers in highly regarded, peer-reviewed journals. She frequently speaks at international conferences and was awarded the prestigious Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Julie lives with her family in a smart home she designed herself.", "probe_text": "Julie Wong stands out as an eminent researcher in Robotics and Artificial Intelligence. Over 50 of her papers, either authored or co-authored by her, have been featured in well-respected, peer-reviewed publications. She is a regular speaker at global conferences and received the distinguished Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Her residence, which she shares with her family, is a smart home of her own design.", "target_level": "a2", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_TSAR_20-b1_3acbec9b", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "TSAR_20-b1", "base_text": "Julie Wong is a leading researcher in the field of Robotics and AI. She has been published extensively as author and co-author of over 50 papers in highly regarded, peer-reviewed journals. She frequently speaks at international conferences and was awarded the prestigious Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Julie lives with her family in a smart home she designed herself.", "probe_text": "Julie Wong stands out as an eminent researcher in Robotics and Artificial Intelligence. Over 50 of her papers, authored or co-authored, have appeared in well-respected, peer-reviewed publications. She is a regular speaker at global conferences and received the distinguished Royal Academy Simone Fielding fellowship in 2012. Living with her family, Julie inhabits a smart home that she designed on her own.", "target_level": "b1", "dataset": "TSAR2025", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_ASSET_test_0000_e0fc441f", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0000", "base_text": "One side of the armed conflicts is composed mainly of the Sudanese military and the Janjaweed, a Sudanese militia group recruited mostly from the Afro-Arab Abbala tribes of the northern Rizeigat region in Sudan.", "probe_text": "The Sudanese military and the Janjaweed, a militia predominantly made up of Afro-Arab Abbala tribespeople from Sudan's northern Rizeigat area, form one side of the armed conflicts.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
{"probe_id": "patch_sem_qwen2_5_32b_ASSET_test_0001_1aa469c6", "probe_family": "invariance", "probe_subtype": "semantic", "generator_model": "qwen2.5-32b", "base_item_id": "ASSET_test_0001", "base_text": "Jeddah is the principal gateway to Mecca, Islam's holiest city, which able-bodied Muslims are required to visit at least once in their lifetime.", "probe_text": "Jeddah serves as the main entry point to Mecca, the most sacred city in Islam, where every physically capable Muslim is obligated to make a pilgrimage at least once during their life.", "target_level": "simpler", "dataset": "ASSET", "tier": 1, "source": "patch_v1"}
